RE-MEASURED AT HEAD 192360e; I also raised this slice from the audit register at the same HEAD, so the evidence is first-hand.

DEFECT ONE, CONFIRMED, and the adjacent line proves it is a copy-paste rather than a decision. In scripts/release/supply-chain.mjs, inside runReleaseSupplyChain:
  const tools = await ports.observeTools({ repositoryRoot: String(input.repositoryRoot) });
  if (!tools) return releaseRefusal("FROZEN_INSTALL_FAILED");                      <-- WRONG SUBSYSTEM
  if (tools.node !== "24.16.0" || tools.pnpm !== "11.0.8" || tools.cdxgen !== "12.8.2") return releaseRefusal("TOOLCHAIN_IDENTITY_MISMATCH");   <-- correct family, right there
The genuine frozen-install refusal is a DIFFERENT site further down: `const installed = await ports.frozenInstall({ sourceRoot: root }); if (installed.exitCode !== 0) return releaseRefusal("FROZEN_INSTALL_FAILED");`. Two unrelated conditions share one reason while the toolchain family already has its own.

REFUSAL SHAPE MATTERS FOR THE TESTS. release-subject.mjs:67-74 `releaseRefusal(reason)` returns {code: RELEASE_SUPPLY_CHAIN_CODE, ok:false, reason, refusedBy: RELEASE_SUPPLY_CHAIN_LAYER}. `code` is the SAME constant for every refusal in this subsystem, so asserting `code` is vacuous here - `reason` is the discriminator, `refusedBy` is the layer. Any test that pins only `code` would pass before and after this fix.

RELEASE_REFUSAL_REASONS is a frozen 19-member array at release-subject.mjs:22-33 and already contains TOOLCHAIN_IDENTITY_MISMATCH, so a toolchain-observation member sits naturally beside it. IMPORTANT AND OUT OF SCOPE: releaseRefusal's parameter is a plain `/** @type {string} */ reason` with NO validation against that array, so the frozen set is documentation, not enforcement - a typo'd reason would pass silently. Noted for a future slice, deliberately not fixed here.

DEFECT TWO, CONFIRMED. The function ends:
  } catch { return releaseRefusal("EVIDENCE_WRITE_INTERRUPTED"); }
  finally { process.off("SIGINT", stop); process.off("SIGTERM", stop); cleanRoots(roots); }
cleanRoots (defined just above runReleaseSupplyChain) is `for (const root of roots.splice(0)) { rmSync(root, {force:true, recursive:true}); rmSync(`${root}.tar`, {force:true}); }`. A throw from a finally block replaces the return value and escapes the surrounding catch, so a Windows EBUSY on a held handle converts a truthful success or a specific refusal into an unhandled rmSync error. `force: true` does not help: it suppresses ENOENT, not EBUSY. The SIGINT/SIGTERM `stop` handler also calls cleanRoots before process.exit(130), so that path has the same exposure.

SIZES: supply-chain.mjs is 256 physical lines - ALREADY ABOVE the 250 target - and release-subject.mjs is 224. That is why the cleanup fix belongs inside cleanRoots rather than as a wrapper around the finally. Test file exists: tests/integration/release-supply-chain.test.mjs.
